WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:01.719
HILLS: Ah, we've got a wonderful crowd here.

00:00:01.719 --> 00:00:02.719
Welcome, one and all.

00:00:02.719 --> 00:00:07.970
I'm Carla Hills, and I co-chair the Council
on Foreign Relations, and I feel very privileged

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:12.850
today to introduce our outstanding guest.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:18.570
And I know you've been told to turn off your
cellphones and so forth, so I'll go in and

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:28.929
just tell you some few facts about Governor
Lael Brainard, who became a member of the

00:00:28.929 --> 00:00:35.450
Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve
System in June of 2014, and her term will

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:40.160
end in January of 2026.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.490
For four years, prior to her appointment to
the board, she served as an undersecretary

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:51.490
at the U.S. Department of Treasury and as
counsel to the secretary of the treasury.

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:57.460
And in those roles, she was the U.S. representative
to the G-20 financial deputies, and to the

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:03.560
G-7 financial deputies, and was a member of
the Financial Stability Board.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:10.600
She was awarded the Alexander Hamilton Award
for her splendid service.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:17.030
And she also served as deputy national economic
advisor and deputy assistant to President

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:23.229
Clinton, and was his personal representative
at both the G-7 and the G-8.

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:27.950
And prior to her government service, she served
as vice president and as founding director

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:33.950
of the Global Economic and Development Program
at Brookings Institution, where she built

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:39.229
a research program to address global economic
challenges.

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:46.710
In the 1990s, before she came to Washington,
she was assistant and associate professor

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:53.450
of applied economics at the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology, and she published

00:01:53.450 --> 00:02:00.920
a number of articles and edited a number of
books that were extremely well-received.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:09.509
She earned her Bachelor's from Wesleyan University,
and her Master's and her doctorate from Harvard

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:14.580
University in economics, and she was a White
House fellow.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:20.530
So it gives me great pleasure to give this
very special guest of ours the podium.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:27.950
And so, Lael Brainard, please.

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:31.000
(Applause.)

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:40.580
BRAINARD: Well, I want to thank Ambassador
Hills for that very kind introduction, and

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.930
it's a real pleasure to be here today at the
Council on Foreign Relations.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:49.650
In recent months, financial conditions have
eased, and there are encouraging signs that

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.910
domestic consumption has regained momentum.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:58.610
But today's tentative signs of labor markets
slowing remind us that we cannot take the

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:02.370
resilience of our recovery for granted.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:07.780
In today's circumstances, prudent risk management
implies we should wait for additional data

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:14.980
to provide confidence that domestic activity
has rebounded, and reassurance that near-term

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:19.080
international events will not derail progress
toward our goals.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.220
As we consider the appropriate posture of
policy, the most immediate question facing

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:28.530
us is whether the data provide confidence
that domestic economic activity has strengthened

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:32.480
notably following two disappointing quarters.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.629
Today's labor market report is sobering, and
suggests that the labor market has slowed.

00:03:37.629 --> 00:03:45.540
Nonfarm payroll employment increased at an
average monthly pace of 116,000 over the last

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:52.830
three months, well below the 220,000 per month
average pace over the preceding 12 months.

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:58.550
Although the unemployment rate moved lower,
to 4.7 percent, involuntary part-time employment

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:03.269
increased and labor force participation declined.

00:04:03.269 --> 00:04:08.140
The recent news on inflation, the second leg
of our dual mandate, has also been mixed.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:12.770
The price of oil has rebounded significantly
from the lows reached earlier in the year

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:18.570
on expectations that supply and demand are
likely to be in closer alignment.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:24.770
The dollar has receded a bit on net from its
peak in January, though it's still about 15

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:29.460
percent above the level in mid-2014 in real
effective terms.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:36.970
As a result, non-oil import prices look likely
to stabilize this quarter after a year and

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:38.550
a half of declines.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:43.810
These developments coincided with the easing
of financial conditions since mid-February.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:49.770
Thus the conditions supporting further progress
on inflation are likely due at least in part

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:55.720
to expectations of more gradual U.S. monetary
policy, and could diminish if those expectations

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:58.889
were to materially shift.

00:04:58.889 --> 00:05:02.910
While there are thus signs that inflation
will move higher over the medium term, measures

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:07.810
of core inflation have yet to convincingly
exceed the low levels that have prevailed

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:09.979
over much of the recovery.

00:05:09.979 --> 00:05:15.680
The 12-month change in core PCE prices was
only 1.6 percent in April, which is roughly

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:22.180
equal to the average change from the end of
2019 to-from the end of 2009 to the end of

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:26.949
2014, which is noticeably below our target
of 2 percent.

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:32.060
We can't rule out that this stubbornly low
inflation may be having an effect also on

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:34.930
inflation expectations.

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:40.160
Market-based measures of inflation compensation
remain extremely low, with the five-year,

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:45.699
five-year forward currently around 1.5 percent,
which is 1 1/4 percentage points below levels

00:05:45.699 --> 00:05:49.680
prevailing prior to mid-2014.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.509
In addition, some survey-based measures, such
as the Michigan Consumer Survey, also remain

00:05:54.509 --> 00:05:56.250
below historical norms.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:01.680
So, in short, although some developments point
to a possible firming inflation, the persistently

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:07.150
low level of inflation, together with signs
of a deterioration in inflation expectations,

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:11.280
suggests that the risks are weighted to the
downside.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.870
Further progress towards our goals, which
are full employment and 2 percent inflation,

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:20.360
will depend importantly, therefore, on a rebound
in growth.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:25.470
Following disappointing GDP growth that averaged
only 1.1 percent over the past two quarters,

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:31.750
I've been very focused on incoming data, especially
on consumption, which points to a pickup in

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:34.240
growth this quarter.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:40.050
Consumer expenditures rose strongly at 0.6
percent in April, and auto sales edged higher

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:41.430
in May.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:46.160
Nonetheless, the data relevant for gauging
second-quarter growth are still relatively

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:47.500
sparse.

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:52.729
In general, demand growth in recent quarters
has benefited from relatively strong households

00:06:52.729 --> 00:06:58.610
who are buoyed by strong employment, reduced
oil prices, and low interest rates, but have

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:03.100
been pulled down by weak business investment
and exports.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:07.889
While consumption and housing investment more
than accounts for the 2 percent increase in

00:07:07.889 --> 00:07:14.139
GDP over the past four quarters, business
investment and net exports together subtracted

00:07:14.139 --> 00:07:16.130
on net 1/4 percentage point.

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:21.759
Of course, the rise in the dollar and the
decline in foreign growth reduced demand for

00:07:21.759 --> 00:07:28.320
American exports, as well as profits and investment
at U.S. firms, along with the declines in

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:29.360
the price of oil.

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:34.400
So, if we look at the 12 months ending in
April, manufacturing output increased only

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:41.550
0.4 percent, while total industrial production
fell 1.1 percent, which, of course, also includes

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:46.880
drilling of and extraction of oil and gas.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:52.390
Of course, if the easing in financial conditions
since mid-February, and the firming of oil

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:57.919
prices, and the stabilization of the dollar
were to continue, business investment and

00:07:57.919 --> 00:07:59.310
exporters would benefit.

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:05.460
Of course, there are risks to the projection
that future GDP growth will be strong enough

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.810
to deliver further progress on inflation and
employment.

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:14.870
Most immediately, there is material uncertainty
surrounding the United Kingdom's June 23rd

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:15.990
Brexit referendum.

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:21.860
The IMF has noted that a vote in favor of
Brexit could unsettle financial markets and

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:29.360
create a period of uncertainty while the relationship
between the U.K. and EU is renegotiated.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:34.899
Although it is very difficult to forecast
the economic effects, we can't rule out a

00:08:34.899 --> 00:08:40.339
significant adverse reaction in the near term,
such as a substantial jump in financial risk

00:08:40.339 --> 00:08:42.259
premiums.

00:08:42.259 --> 00:08:46.790
Because international financial markets are
tightly linked, an adverse reaction in European

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:52.069
financial markets could affect U.S. financial
markets and, of course, through them economic

00:08:52.069 --> 00:08:54.199
activity here.

00:08:54.199 --> 00:09:00.110
We also cannot dismiss the possible reemergence
of risk surrounding China and emerging markets

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:01.899
more broadly.

00:09:01.899 --> 00:09:07.619
In recent months, capital outflows in China
have moderated as the dollar has stabilized

00:09:07.619 --> 00:09:11.569
and pressures on China's exchange rate have
eased.

00:09:11.569 --> 00:09:16.269
Should pressures through the exchange-rate
channel reemerge, we can't rule out a recurrence

00:09:16.269 --> 00:09:22.970
of financial stress, which could affect not
only China but, again, other emerging markets

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:28.329
that are linked by China via supply chains
or commodity exports, and ultimately to conditions

00:09:28.329 --> 00:09:29.329
here.

00:09:29.329 --> 00:09:34.589
China's making a challenging transition from
export- to domestic demand-led growth, and

00:09:34.589 --> 00:09:41.069
the cost of reallocating resources from excess
capacity sectors to more dynamic sectors could

00:09:41.069 --> 00:09:44.480
further impair growth in the near term.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:50.119
While China has taken steps to limit the extent
of the slowdown, there's an evident tension

00:09:50.119 --> 00:09:55.660
in policy between reform and stimulus, and
the effect of the stimulus may already be

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:56.660
waning.

00:09:56.660 --> 00:10:01.279
Vulnerabilities, such as excess capacity,
elevated corporate debt, and risks in the

00:10:01.279 --> 00:10:07.009
shadow banking sector, appear to be building
and could pose continued risks over the medium

00:10:07.009 --> 00:10:09.179
term.

00:10:09.179 --> 00:10:13.929
The broader fragility of the global economic
environment is unlikely to resolve anytime

00:10:13.929 --> 00:10:14.929
soon.

00:10:14.929 --> 00:10:19.720
Growth in the advanced economies remains dependent
on extraordinary unconventional monetary policy

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:25.819
accommodation, while conventional policy continues
to be constrained by the zero lower bound.

00:10:25.819 --> 00:10:30.820
Conventional policy's efficacy is more tested
and better understood than unconventional

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:36.579
policy, and it can respond readily to any
upside surprises, but presently constrained

00:10:36.579 --> 00:10:39.220
in adjusting to downside surprises.

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:46.360
This asymmetry in the ability of policy effectively
skews risks to the outlook to the downside,

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:50.179
and may amplify the sensitivity of the exchange
rate.

00:10:50.179 --> 00:10:55.399
The evidence suggests that over the past year
dollar exchange-rate movements have become

00:10:55.399 --> 00:11:03.029
considerably more sensitive in response to
monetary policy surprises than they were previously.

00:11:03.029 --> 00:11:07.360
That's also consistent with recent research
which suggests that cross-border financial

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:12.610
transmission is likely to be amplified at
near-zero interest rates, where the ability

00:11:12.610 --> 00:11:17.920
to provide additional support through domestic
channels is limited.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:22.649
In this environment, markets have become quite
sensitive to the possibility of a prolonged

00:11:22.649 --> 00:11:29.629
period of low growth, low inflation, and economic
underperformance, as we can see in the current

00:11:29.629 --> 00:11:35.480
negative term premium for the 10-year Treasury
note, or the difference between the yield

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:40.649
on the 10-year Treasury and expected risk-free
short rates over the next 10 years.

00:11:40.649 --> 00:11:46.329
That is a big change from the period prior
to the Great Recession.

00:11:46.329 --> 00:11:52.470
So, as we look across these conditions, while
the easing in conditions, in financial conditions

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:56.339
in particular, since mid-February is very
welcome, it's important to recognize that

00:11:56.339 --> 00:12:02.449
some of the conditions underlying recent bouts
of turmoil largely remain in place, and an

00:12:02.449 --> 00:12:09.660
important reason for the fading of this turbulence
was the expectation of more gradual U.S. monetary

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:10.660
policy.

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:16.209
Should an event trigger renewed fears about
global growth or a reassessment of the policy

00:12:16.209 --> 00:12:22.559
reaction function here in the United States,
financial stress could well return.

00:12:22.559 --> 00:12:27.059
Recognizing that the data we have on hand
for the second quarter is quite mixed and

00:12:27.059 --> 00:12:32.660
still limited, and there's important near-term
uncertainty, there would appear to be an advantage

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:37.489
to waiting until developments provide greater
confidence.

00:12:37.489 --> 00:12:42.170
Prudent risk management would suggest the
risks from waiting until the totality of the

00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:47.209
data provides greater confidence in a rebound
in domestic activity, and there's greater

00:12:47.209 --> 00:12:52.829
certainty regarding the Brexit vote, seem
lower than the risks associated with moving

00:12:52.829 --> 00:12:54.480
ahead of those developments.

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:58.350
That's especially true since the feedback
loop through exchange rate and financial market

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:02.259
channels appears to be elevated.

00:13:02.259 --> 00:13:07.079
In light of this amplified feedback loop,
when conditions are appropriate for a policy

00:13:07.079 --> 00:13:11.429
move, it'll be important that it be understood
that any subsequent moves would be conditioned

00:13:11.429 --> 00:13:16.009
on still further evidence confirming continued
progress towards our objectives, and not as

00:13:16.009 --> 00:13:19.910
inevitable steps on a preset course.

00:13:19.910 --> 00:13:26.160
Indeed, the appropriate path to return monetary
policy to a neutral stance could turn out

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:32.079
to be quite shallow and gradual in the medium
term because it increasingly appears likely

00:13:32.079 --> 00:13:36.990
that the medium-term neutral rate, or the
real federal funds rate that would be consistent

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:42.499
with the economy remaining at full employment
and 2 percent inflation, may be quite low.

00:13:42.499 --> 00:13:46.009
Earlier in the recovery, it seemed likely
that the low level of the natural rate was

00:13:46.009 --> 00:13:51.410
largely due to temporary or cyclical factors,
such as tight credit, weak consumer confidence,

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:54.699
and the loss in household wealth from the
crisis.

00:13:54.699 --> 00:14:00.589
However, the recovery is now well into its
seventh full year, credit in most markets

00:14:00.589 --> 00:14:05.709
is widely available, while consumer confidence
and household net worth are at high levels.

00:14:05.709 --> 00:14:10.209
As a result, it now appears more likely that
much of the decline in the neutral rate is

00:14:10.209 --> 00:14:15.059
likely to prove persistent, consistent with
a variety of estimates.

00:14:15.059 --> 00:14:20.209
With productivity running very low, substantial
overcapacity and disinflationary pressures

00:14:20.209 --> 00:14:26.470
abroad, and less favorable demographics, the
medium-term neutral rate may be lower than

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:28.679
previously anticipated.

00:14:28.679 --> 00:14:35.419
And this may mean that today's federal funds
rate is closer to neutral than previously

00:14:35.419 --> 00:14:36.869
expected.

00:14:36.869 --> 00:14:40.669
Of course, one likely explanation for the
persistence of the low neutral rate is the

00:14:40.669 --> 00:14:44.579
sharp drop-off in potential growth since the
Great Recession.

00:14:44.579 --> 00:14:50.630
So if you look at the period from '53 to 2003,
potential output growth varied between 3 and

00:14:50.630 --> 00:14:54.679
4 1/2 percent, with one very brief exception.

00:14:54.679 --> 00:14:58.540
Over the recovery, it's averaged only 1 1/4
percent.

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:02.399
One contributor to this decline has been a
reduction in the labor force participation

00:15:02.399 --> 00:15:07.910
rate due to aging, but another has been a
marked slowing in productivity growth.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:13.489
Over the six years from the end of 2009 to
the end of 2015, productivity grew only a

00:15:13.489 --> 00:15:18.879
little over 1/2 percent per year, compared
with average growth of 2 1/4 percent over

00:15:18.879 --> 00:15:22.149
the 50 years prior to the Great Recession.

00:15:22.149 --> 00:15:26.109
The reasons for such a dramatic slowing are
still not clear.

00:15:26.109 --> 00:15:32.740
So, in summary, recent economic developments
have been mixed, and important downside risks

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:33.740
remain.

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:37.759
In this environment, prudent risk management
suggests there's a benefit to waiting for

00:15:37.759 --> 00:15:42.769
additional data to provide confidence that
domestic activity has rebounded strongly,

00:15:42.769 --> 00:15:48.859
and reassurance that near-term international
events will not derail progress.

00:15:48.859 --> 00:15:53.239
In addition, because the depressed level of
the neutral rate of interest is likely to

00:15:53.239 --> 00:15:58.649
reflect forces that are persistent, the appropriate
path of policy is likely to remain quite shallow

00:15:58.649 --> 00:16:00.369
for several years.

00:16:00.369 --> 00:16:08.189
Thank you.

00:16:08.189 --> 00:16:16.009
(Applause.)

00:16:16.009 --> 00:16:46.449
HILLS: Well, that was a wonderful presentation,
and you raised a lot of questions that I want

00:16:46.449 --> 00:16:47.449
to ask you.

00:16:47.449 --> 00:16:54.519
And I'm going to start by taking a few minutes
to talk about some of the issues, and then

00:16:54.519 --> 00:16:58.850
turn it over to all of you to come up with
questions.

00:16:58.850 --> 00:17:08.079
And let me start with saying talk a little
bit about how you factor in exchange rates

00:17:08.079 --> 00:17:10.410
into policy recommendations.

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:19.039
BRAINARD: So the international environment
has been extremely salient over the last really

00:17:19.039 --> 00:17:21.819
now two years in particular.

00:17:21.819 --> 00:17:27.579
The United States economy is tightly linked
to the rest of the world, obviously through

00:17:27.579 --> 00:17:30.039
trade, but also through financial linkages.

00:17:30.039 --> 00:17:35.070
And some of that transmission takes place
through changes in the exchange rate.

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:45.490
What we've seen since the middle of 2014 is
a very significant increase in the U.S. dollar

00:17:45.490 --> 00:17:53.009
exchange rate, in part reflecting expectations
that conditions would warrant a bigger divergence

00:17:53.009 --> 00:17:56.730
in policy between the United States and some
of our foreign partners.

00:17:56.730 --> 00:18:02.820
But, of course, that change in the exchange
rate, that tightening in financial conditions,

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:09.370
then affects our exporters, it affects our
import prices, which affects core inflation

00:18:09.370 --> 00:18:10.370
here.

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:17.279
And so it, in turn, has a bit of a depressing
effect on U.S. economic activity, which front-runs

00:18:17.279 --> 00:18:21.500
in some respects U.S. monetary policy.

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:31.429
HILLS: I was reading that an economist, Dr.
Bill Cline, calculates that a 10 percent appreciation

00:18:31.429 --> 00:18:40.010
of the dollar worsens the U.S. account-current
account position by about 1.7 percent, and

00:18:40.010 --> 00:18:46.639
the trade-weighted average of the dollar has
appreciated 25 percent in the last three years

00:18:46.639 --> 00:18:48.909
alone.

00:18:48.909 --> 00:18:55.500
How do you see the current account deficit
as a factor in the Fed's thinking?

00:18:55.500 --> 00:19:01.919
BRAINARD: Well, we definitely do see that
net exports have been a drag on the economy

00:19:01.919 --> 00:19:08.620
since June of 2014 when we started to see
that expectation of policy diversion showing

00:19:08.620 --> 00:19:13.240
up in a sharp appreciation of the dollar.

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:20.320
And so, you know, you see that in a way that
is particularly evident in manufacturing,

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:26.399
but you see it in net exports more generally
that continue to be a drag today if you look

00:19:26.399 --> 00:19:28.340
at the data.

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:35.279
And of course, that is part of the reason
that we have to be very, very careful in protecting

00:19:35.279 --> 00:19:42.600
and preserving the progress we are making
here at home because in the United States,

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:51.540
while consumption makes up a very large portion
of the overall aggregate growth, it's not

00:19:51.540 --> 00:20:00.740
strong enough really to carry the burden of
global growth in a world where globally aggregate

00:20:00.740 --> 00:20:02.740
demand is quite weak.

00:20:02.740 --> 00:20:10.790
HILLS: You know, you mentioned in your remarks,
I would paraphrase them by saying take it

00:20:10.790 --> 00:20:19.059
step by step, and yet some economists are
arguing quite vehemently that we should raise

00:20:19.059 --> 00:20:25.919
interest rates lest we create a bubble and
the problem of tomorrow.

00:20:25.919 --> 00:20:31.280
Give some information about how we would respond
to those economists.

00:20:31.280 --> 00:20:39.009
BRAINARD: So I think if you look across the
set of economic conditions that we take into

00:20:39.009 --> 00:20:47.090
account as we assess what the appropriate
stance of monetary policy is, you know, we

00:20:47.090 --> 00:20:52.280
have very specific goals of full employment
and 2 percent inflation.

00:20:52.280 --> 00:21:00.809
And while we've made considerable progress
on the employment leg of our mandate as we've

00:21:00.809 --> 00:21:08.690
seen from today's, even on the labor market
we've seen some slowing in recent months.

00:21:08.690 --> 00:21:15.409
And of course, on the inflation leg of our
mandate we are still and have been persistently

00:21:15.409 --> 00:21:17.990
short of our 2 percent target.

00:21:17.990 --> 00:21:27.320
So under those circumstances and given the
very weak overall aggregate demand globally,

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:33.690
the appropriate path of policy has been to
continue to be very supportive of demand growth

00:21:33.690 --> 00:21:40.090
here in the U.S.
Looking forward, again, you know, as I see

00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:47.539
it, this is my take on it, current circumstances
would dictate a risk management approach.

00:21:47.539 --> 00:21:52.789
There are important risks in the international
environment, most immediately Brexit, but

00:21:52.789 --> 00:21:59.340
there are also risks associated with China
and the broader emerging market complex.

00:21:59.340 --> 00:22:06.920
And there's also a fairly incomplete and mixed
picture to give us a sense of how strong the

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:08.500
second quarter is.

00:22:08.500 --> 00:22:14.730
In that context, I think prudence suggests
that the risks of waiting are lower than the

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:18.679
risks of moving ahead preemptively.

00:22:18.679 --> 00:22:26.779
HILLS: You mentioned Brexit and China, and
then Europe is slow.

00:22:26.779 --> 00:22:32.990
Give me some good news that makes you think
that-(laughter) -that the future could be

00:22:32.990 --> 00:22:33.990
brighter.

00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:40.700
BRAINARD: So American consumers are still
relatively confident.

00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:43.690
Their incomes have been improving.

00:22:43.690 --> 00:22:51.299
And you know, the consumer confidence indicators
are still relatively positive.

00:22:51.299 --> 00:22:59.391
So that is a very important bright spot that
we want to carefully support and protect.

00:22:59.391 --> 00:23:05.320
And again, you know, we have seen an important
material easing in financial conditions that

00:23:05.320 --> 00:23:10.809
are more supportive, likely to be more supportive
of our manufacturers and our exporters and

00:23:10.809 --> 00:23:16.150
of business investment than certainly conditions
were earlier in this year.

00:23:16.150 --> 00:23:23.779
HILLS: And you mentioned that, you know, the
jobs report today, 38,000 was a disappointment.

00:23:23.779 --> 00:23:31.679
The U.S. banks reported their first quarter
and they're down 1.9 percent.

00:23:31.679 --> 00:23:34.549
Is that also something that causes you concern?

00:23:34.549 --> 00:23:41.490
BRAINARD: So we in taking into account the
sort of strength of likely business investment

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:47.309
and the business sector, corporate earnings
are very important.

00:23:47.309 --> 00:23:56.159
And obviously there has been some evidence
that both the relatively elevated level of

00:23:56.159 --> 00:24:05.080
the exchange rate or the more elevated relative
to what it was in 2014 on top of the very

00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:11.340
sharp decline in energy prices that we had
seen together have depressed corporate earnings.

00:24:11.340 --> 00:24:17.429
And that is a very important indicator for
business investment going forward.

00:24:17.429 --> 00:24:22.080
And you know, business investment is important,
not just for growth, but also for productivity.

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:26.820
And I mentioned earlier that the numbers there,
you know, are puzzlingly and disappointingly

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:27.820
very low.

00:24:27.820 --> 00:24:33.440
HILLS: You know, there are vacancies on the
board of the Fed.

00:24:33.440 --> 00:24:38.480
And is that a concern?

00:24:38.480 --> 00:24:43.769
BRAINARD: It would be great if we were at
full strength of seven rather than being at

00:24:43.769 --> 00:24:47.480
five, absolutely, that would be very, very
welcome.

00:24:47.480 --> 00:24:55.840
HILLS: And explain a little bit about you've
got 12 regional Federal Reserve boards.

00:24:55.840 --> 00:25:03.179
Talk a little bit about their role and how
they fit into your responsibilities dealing

00:25:03.179 --> 00:25:07.279
with inflation and employment, looking at
those as-

00:25:07.279 --> 00:25:08.279
BRAINARD: Yeah.

00:25:08.279 --> 00:25:13.750
So the way that the Federal Reserve system
is set up, the Federal Open Markets Committee,

00:25:13.750 --> 00:25:22.240
which is the committee that meets periodically
to adjust and assess monetary policy, includes

00:25:22.240 --> 00:25:29.999
12 regional presidents and should include
seven board members here in Washington.

00:25:29.999 --> 00:25:39.269
And you know, the 12 board presidents are
supposed to and do bring their own independent

00:25:39.269 --> 00:25:42.710
assessment of economic conditions.

00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:48.049
Probably when the Federal Reserve was created
and, you know, people had to travel across

00:25:48.049 --> 00:25:53.440
the country from the 12th district more slowly
than they do today and data was not as widely

00:25:53.440 --> 00:25:59.169
available on national conditions, they probably
were very focused on their own districts,

00:25:59.169 --> 00:26:02.690
which was critically important for assessing
the overall U.S. economy.

00:26:02.690 --> 00:26:11.110
Today they bring really important information
from their districts, but we all look at the

00:26:11.110 --> 00:26:12.889
same data.

00:26:12.889 --> 00:26:18.990
And so the Fed deliberations, I think, is
a very rich set of deliberations among the

00:26:18.990 --> 00:26:25.510
board members, who each have their own assessments
as well, and the presidents, led by the chair.

00:26:25.510 --> 00:26:32.190
HILLS: Well, you can see that we're very fortunate
to have the governor where she is today.

00:26:32.190 --> 00:26:37.320
And we're certainly fortunate to have her
on the podium, so let me turn it over to you

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:38.499
to ask questions.

00:26:38.499 --> 00:26:46.470
And I would ask you to state your name and
state your question.

00:26:46.470 --> 00:26:50.149
Q: Thank you for being here.

00:26:50.149 --> 00:26:51.149
Rosita Najmi.

00:26:51.149 --> 00:26:54.539
I'm on the Financial Services for the Poor
Team at the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation.

00:26:54.539 --> 00:27:01.830
I was really curious about if at all, if it's
on the radar, of the issue of de-risking.

00:27:01.830 --> 00:27:08.090
So we've been looking really closely at cross-border
flows, so ranging from remittances, correspondent

00:27:08.090 --> 00:27:13.080
banking, humanitarian aid and trade finance
as well, and the kind of unintended consequences

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:17.980
on the poor when these flows are being cut
off or when the cost of capital is raised.

00:27:17.980 --> 00:27:19.580
I was just curious, you know, we've been collecting
stories.

00:27:19.580 --> 00:27:24.960
Some central banks in certain markets are
actually considering filling in the role of

00:27:24.960 --> 00:27:28.029
the correspondent relations for the respondent
banks.

00:27:28.029 --> 00:27:31.840
We're also seeing some banks figuring out
what the willingness to pay is to maintain

00:27:31.840 --> 00:27:33.340
correspondent relations.

00:27:33.340 --> 00:27:37.320
Any thoughts in terms of what the role could
be for the Fed?

00:27:37.320 --> 00:27:40.710
Or any other just high-level thoughts on this
issue on how we could be supportive would

00:27:40.710 --> 00:27:41.710
be helpful.

00:27:41.710 --> 00:27:46.960
BRAINARD: So on the question of how banks
can continue to maintain their international

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:55.210
correspondent banking relationships while
also fulfilling their obligations under BSA/AML

00:27:55.210 --> 00:27:59.990
and doing so very fully is an important issue
for us.

00:27:59.990 --> 00:28:09.549
We obviously do want to see those international
relationships being able to provide the necessary

00:28:09.549 --> 00:28:15.639
financial services to allow people to send
remittances back home or do a host of business

00:28:15.639 --> 00:28:17.980
kinds of transactions.

00:28:17.980 --> 00:28:24.919
And so it's an area that we have had conversations
with some of the banks that we supervise,

00:28:24.919 --> 00:28:31.380
but also we're in close communication with
the Treasury Department that sets some of

00:28:31.380 --> 00:28:33.139
those rules.

00:28:33.139 --> 00:28:43.519
And you know, our hope is that the environment
will, you know, become a more sort of supportive

00:28:43.519 --> 00:28:44.679
environment going forward.

00:28:44.679 --> 00:28:49.559
I think banks can do what they need to do
for BSA/AML and still maintain those relationships.

00:28:49.559 --> 00:28:54.590
And I think there's also some developments
in the technology space, the technology arena

00:28:54.590 --> 00:29:02.480
that holds some promise of making those linkages
perhaps cheaper, more real-time, and also

00:29:02.480 --> 00:29:03.480
safe.

00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:06.130
HILLS: Yes, up front here.

00:29:06.130 --> 00:29:07.130
Q: Hi.

00:29:07.130 --> 00:29:09.510
Jon Colby from The Carlyle Group.

00:29:09.510 --> 00:29:12.549
It's been reported that the five largest technology
companies in the U.S., for example, are sitting

00:29:12.549 --> 00:29:15.610
on $500 billion of cash on their balance sheets.

00:29:15.610 --> 00:29:19.240
What does that look like to you from your
perspective and historically?

00:29:19.240 --> 00:29:24.559
And then what do you think it's going to take
to have some of that come unstuck and increase

00:29:24.559 --> 00:29:25.559
productivity?

00:29:25.559 --> 00:29:36.119
BRAINARD: So this question of how much different
kinds of firms, you know, have incentives

00:29:36.119 --> 00:29:40.970
to put their cash and their capital to work
I think is one that's extremely important

00:29:40.970 --> 00:29:47.840
for the strength of business investment, for
growth more generally.

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:56.590
And that's on the short-term part of the ledger,
and then, of course, medium to long term for

00:29:56.590 --> 00:29:59.259
the productive potential of the U.S. economy.

00:29:59.259 --> 00:30:04.269
So it's certainly an area that we have a lot
of interest in.

00:30:04.269 --> 00:30:09.059
In terms of the tools that we have, obviously,
you know, we've been very supportive, I think,

00:30:09.059 --> 00:30:17.361
in terms of the stance of monetary policy
has been supportive of activity in the business

00:30:17.361 --> 00:30:20.450
sector, activity in the household sector.

00:30:20.450 --> 00:30:25.529
There are other policy considerations that
may be relevant that just are well outside

00:30:25.529 --> 00:30:29.600
of our realm or our influence.

00:30:29.600 --> 00:30:36.629
But certainly this issue of business investment
is an important one, and trying to create

00:30:36.629 --> 00:30:43.409
the best possible environment for that investment
to take place is really where we come into

00:30:43.409 --> 00:30:44.850
that equation.

00:30:44.850 --> 00:30:48.409
HILLS: Yes, third table back.

00:30:48.409 --> 00:30:53.660
Q: Bill Courtney, RAND Corporation.

00:30:53.660 --> 00:31:00.220
The eurozone and Japan appear to be over-relying
on monetary policy and under-relying on structural

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:01.280
economic reform.

00:31:01.280 --> 00:31:02.600
Is that risky?

00:31:02.600 --> 00:31:10.110
And are there any prospects that the eurozone
and Japan will gather the political will to

00:31:10.110 --> 00:31:12.130
undertake more structural economic reform?

00:31:12.130 --> 00:31:22.049
BRAINARD: Well, I think obviously we have
a common interest, the United States, Japan,

00:31:22.049 --> 00:31:30.739
euro area, larger markets, China, in seeing
a broad set of policies in each economic area

00:31:30.739 --> 00:31:34.279
that supports global aggregate demand more
broadly.

00:31:34.279 --> 00:31:41.860
And you know, in each economy, that would
entail a somewhat different mix of fiscal

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:44.539
and monetary policies as well as structural
policies.

00:31:44.539 --> 00:31:48.570
And those are very specific to the circumstances
of the different economies.

00:31:48.570 --> 00:31:58.009
You know, I do think that in today's circumstances
where aggregate demand overall in the world

00:31:58.009 --> 00:32:05.410
seems to be quite weak and deficient, there
is a case for greater fiscal support in some

00:32:05.410 --> 00:32:08.240
economies, so places like the euro area potentially.

00:32:08.240 --> 00:32:14.070
Certainly, we've seen some moves in that direction
in Japan to complement monetary policy.

00:32:14.070 --> 00:32:22.649
And also, you know, there's plenty of potential
for infrastructure investment and productive

00:32:22.649 --> 00:32:29.179
investment that not only would boost short-term
demand, but also would boost the long-term

00:32:29.179 --> 00:32:32.080
supply potential in many economies around
the world.

00:32:32.080 --> 00:32:34.830
So you know, the mix is an important issue.

00:32:34.830 --> 00:32:40.999
It's going to be specific to each economy,
but our interest is to see that, you know,

00:32:40.999 --> 00:32:45.190
everybody's pulling in the same direction
to try to lift aggregate demand.

00:32:45.190 --> 00:32:56.320
There's really no good outcome where policies
are just shifting demand across borders.

00:32:56.320 --> 00:32:59.989
That's, in the end, going to be self-defeating.

00:32:59.989 --> 00:33:00.989
HILLS: Yes, right here.

00:33:00.989 --> 00:33:02.999
Q: Hi, I'm Teresa-
HILLS: Wait for the mic.

00:33:02.999 --> 00:33:03.999
Q: Sorry.

00:33:03.999 --> 00:33:06.369
Teresa Barger from Cartica Capital.

00:33:06.369 --> 00:33:13.279
So I'm just wondering if you all look at the
ratio of inventory to sales, and it's been

00:33:13.279 --> 00:33:21.029
going up very steadily the last 18 months
and now reached almost the peak of the 2008

00:33:21.029 --> 00:33:29.230
spike, and whether that's of concern and whether
you see that as a recessionary signal or not.

00:33:29.230 --> 00:33:40.270
BRAINARD: So the board has incredible economists
who know more about every data series, U.S.

00:33:40.270 --> 00:33:43.370
and foreign, than one could ever ask.

00:33:43.370 --> 00:33:51.730
And inventories is a critically important
focus of, you know, the forecasting staff

00:33:51.730 --> 00:33:57.220
at the board, and is a very important input
into our assessment of, you know, if you see

00:33:57.220 --> 00:34:00.190
weakness in the first quarter or the fourth
quarter.

00:34:00.190 --> 00:34:09.389
How much of that is going to be remediated
by a rebound in the first quarter is importantly

00:34:09.389 --> 00:34:14.639
associated with this question of whether inventory
build was high or low relative to what one

00:34:14.639 --> 00:34:18.149
would have expected in our usual forecasts.

00:34:18.149 --> 00:34:20.889
So it is an area of intense focus.

00:34:20.889 --> 00:34:25.679
I don't think that I would say that it's a,
you know, a clean predictor.

00:34:25.679 --> 00:34:31.510
I think there are very few kind of individual,
clean predictors of, you know, whether the

00:34:31.510 --> 00:34:34.629
economy is strengthening or going into a recession.

00:34:34.629 --> 00:34:39.860
But it is extremely important to try to understand
when we do see some weak quarters, like we

00:34:39.860 --> 00:34:45.679
have, to try to assess whether there may be
some very important temporary factors that

00:34:45.679 --> 00:34:48.480
are going to abate in the following quarter.

00:34:48.480 --> 00:34:58.480
And you know, again, more broadly, I think
we've seen some data on the second quarter,

00:34:58.480 --> 00:35:03.950
but I think it would be extremely important
to have additional data to make a full assessment

00:35:03.950 --> 00:35:07.140
of what the second quarter is going to look
like.

00:35:07.140 --> 00:35:09.040
HILLS: Over here, yeah.

00:35:09.040 --> 00:35:10.450
Q: Hi.

00:35:10.450 --> 00:35:11.880
Nelson Cunningham of McClarty Associates.

00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:16.250
Good to see you, Governor Brainard.

00:35:16.250 --> 00:35:23.109
It's been said in recent years that corporate
profits were at an all-time post-war high

00:35:23.109 --> 00:35:31.460
as a percentage of GDP, whereas employee compensation
was at a post-war low as a percentage of GDP.

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:34.640
Are those measures in the same place today?

00:35:34.640 --> 00:35:40.799
And how do you view what has been higher than
historically normal corporate profits and

00:35:40.799 --> 00:35:44.099
lower than historically normal employee compensation?

00:35:44.099 --> 00:35:52.819
BRAINARD: So in fact, interestingly we have
started to see a bit of an upturn in the labor

00:35:52.819 --> 00:35:54.690
share.

00:35:54.690 --> 00:36:02.520
And as you pointed out, the labor share had
fallen to historically very low levels.

00:36:02.520 --> 00:36:07.890
And we're only seeing the beginnings of tentative
signs of an upturn there.

00:36:07.890 --> 00:36:16.549
But obviously, you know, there's good news
potentially for households and for the domestic

00:36:16.549 --> 00:36:22.670
consumer, potentially good news from a distributive
point of view given what we've seen in terms

00:36:22.670 --> 00:36:27.090
of distributive trends over a very substantial
period of time.

00:36:27.090 --> 00:36:36.260
But there, I think, is too little of a, you
know, sort of trend in this direction so far

00:36:36.260 --> 00:36:42.730
to really make, you know, very firm conclusions
about what we've seen so far.

00:36:42.730 --> 00:36:49.960
HILLS: Way in the back of the room.

00:36:49.960 --> 00:36:52.950
Q: Thank you.

00:36:52.950 --> 00:36:53.950
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

00:36:53.950 --> 00:36:57.339
Lael, I was really taken with your discussion
of the neutral rate.

00:36:57.339 --> 00:37:01.140
You're suggesting that the rest of the world
has kind of imposed an effective cap on how

00:37:01.140 --> 00:37:05.230
far the Fed might be able to go if in fact
the world is what's dragging the neutral rate

00:37:05.230 --> 00:37:06.230
down.

00:37:06.230 --> 00:37:09.109
And what I'm wondering is, what happens on
day two when you all wake up and realize that

00:37:09.109 --> 00:37:12.320
2 percent is about as good as you can get?

00:37:12.320 --> 00:37:16.780
Is that when you start having the discussion
of a higher inflation target or some sort

00:37:16.780 --> 00:37:21.690
of hybridized policy where you'd include other
things other than the interest rate, even

00:37:21.690 --> 00:37:23.150
though you're off zero?

00:37:23.150 --> 00:37:35.630
BRAINARD: So I think right now it's a tentative
kind of observation, but it does appear that

00:37:35.630 --> 00:37:43.970
what we have seen in the estimates to be a
historically very low neutral rate and which

00:37:43.970 --> 00:37:49.480
I think many had assumed was attributable
primarily to cyclical headwinds, it now seems,

00:37:49.480 --> 00:37:56.359
both in some of the estimates, but also just
by watching the evolution of the economy,

00:37:56.359 --> 00:38:03.920
that that neutral rate may well be low, historically
low for some time to come.

00:38:03.920 --> 00:38:09.599
And if that's true, it does mean that we are
closer to neutral today than we thought we

00:38:09.599 --> 00:38:15.130
were or that many people might have thought
we were, which in turn could mean that the

00:38:15.130 --> 00:38:21.390
path of policy, the appropriate path of policy
is likely to be more gradual, more shallow

00:38:21.390 --> 00:38:23.710
over a somewhat longer period of time.

00:38:23.710 --> 00:38:26.569
Now, that's attributable to a host of things.

00:38:26.569 --> 00:38:33.030
And some of that may indeed reflect international
factors.

00:38:33.030 --> 00:38:42.720
It also likely reflects some factors here,
like the aging of our own workforce.

00:38:42.720 --> 00:38:50.099
And as I said earlier, too early to tell,
but productivity, low productivity, could

00:38:50.099 --> 00:38:54.970
also be a piece of that puzzle.

00:38:54.970 --> 00:39:04.140
In terms of what does it mean for policy,
I think what seems to me to be the sort of

00:39:04.140 --> 00:39:12.309
most clear-cut observation is that we're going
to want to engage in a fairly cautious approach,

00:39:12.309 --> 00:39:21.920
a risk management approach, and move cautiously
assessing the effect of any further moves

00:39:21.920 --> 00:39:32.420
as we go, rather than being tied to some preset
course that might be derived from some previous

00:39:32.420 --> 00:39:37.119
tightened cycle that took place under very
different circumstances.

00:39:37.119 --> 00:39:40.250
HILLS: Yes, right here in the middle.

00:39:40.250 --> 00:39:42.140
Q: Hi.

00:39:42.140 --> 00:39:44.020
Nancy Jacklin.

00:39:44.020 --> 00:39:45.910
Hi, Lael.

00:39:45.910 --> 00:39:51.220
This is sort of going back to something that
Carla asked you earlier, which is not just

00:39:51.220 --> 00:39:54.190
do you see any good news, but you talk about
balancing risks.

00:39:54.190 --> 00:39:58.110
And you gave us a pretty good brief on the
downside risks.

00:39:58.110 --> 00:40:01.070
The question is, do you see any upside risks?

00:40:01.070 --> 00:40:06.800
And in looking at that are you considering
any issues in the financial sector, including

00:40:06.800 --> 00:40:12.599
some signs we see of things in the real estate
market now, like house flipping, search for

00:40:12.599 --> 00:40:19.089
yield that's leading to people buying loans
from the peer-to-peer lending that may have

00:40:19.089 --> 00:40:24.260
no credit process attached to them, issues
relating to the amount of derivatives that

00:40:24.260 --> 00:40:27.869
the SEC is seeing being used in retail mutual
funds?

00:40:27.869 --> 00:40:33.710
What are you seeing as if you're looking at
balancing risks, are there upside risks that

00:40:33.710 --> 00:40:35.240
you worry about?

00:40:35.240 --> 00:40:46.180
BRAINARD: So it is certainly a huge focus
of our work at the board, is to carefully,

00:40:46.180 --> 00:40:54.470
systemically, periodically assess risks that
might be building in the financial sector.

00:40:54.470 --> 00:41:05.000
And you know, we have a much more well-developed
analytic framework, and a staff that is now

00:41:05.000 --> 00:41:07.680
dedicated to this in the wake of the crisis.

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:18.339
And we have additional tools that are macro-prudential
in nature that complement our monetary policy

00:41:18.339 --> 00:41:24.369
tools so that, to the extent we do see building
risks in certain sectors, we have a broader

00:41:24.369 --> 00:41:30.970
set of tools that would allow us to, at least
in part, preemptively, ideally, address some

00:41:30.970 --> 00:41:38.369
of those risks, without overly burdening monetary
policy, which already has pretty clear dual

00:41:38.369 --> 00:41:40.609
objectives.

00:41:40.609 --> 00:41:48.089
With regard to the assessment of risks today,
you know, earlier we had called out some concerns

00:41:48.089 --> 00:41:50.780
about leveraged lending and put out some guidance
there.

00:41:50.780 --> 00:41:57.309
And we saw some dissipation and some correction
in that, to some degree.

00:41:57.309 --> 00:42:05.900
I think more recently we have taken a look
at CRE and particularly concentrations of

00:42:05.900 --> 00:42:11.480
CRE in some bank balance sheets as a potential
risk.

00:42:11.480 --> 00:42:21.150
In terms of the real estate market, you know,
there it's hard to generalize, because these

00:42:21.150 --> 00:42:28.720
are-to some degree the developments there
are very specific to different regions.

00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:32.000
But we are very focused.

00:42:32.000 --> 00:42:44.520
There's some signs of a very, you know, extended,
subprime auto set of lending.

00:42:44.520 --> 00:42:51.039
But not to an extent that you would see it
as a macro risk at this point.

00:42:51.039 --> 00:42:57.750
But we are very, very focused on the potential
for risks to build up in particular financial

00:42:57.750 --> 00:43:01.080
sectors, and then to pose risks more broadly.

00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:09.470
And you know, we're much more systematic about
assessing those risks and trying to connect

00:43:09.470 --> 00:43:15.829
those risks to tools that we have at our disposal
to address them.

00:43:15.829 --> 00:43:23.850
HILLS: Yes, in the back of the room, in the
center.

00:43:23.850 --> 00:43:24.850
Q: I'm Haik Gugarats with Argus Media.

00:43:24.850 --> 00:43:32.170
How concerned are you about the asymmetric
effects from the rise of price of oil, in

00:43:32.170 --> 00:43:38.589
the sense that it may nullify some of the
gains in consumer spending we've seen in the

00:43:38.589 --> 00:43:43.319
past year, without boosting business investment
because producers don't seem to be drilling

00:43:43.319 --> 00:43:44.320
more as a result?

00:43:44.320 --> 00:43:45.400
BRAINARD: Yeah.

00:43:45.400 --> 00:43:51.510
So I would say that I can't give you a very
precise answer to that question.

00:43:51.510 --> 00:44:00.350
I mean, as oil prices declined, you know,
I would say our previous historical experiences

00:44:00.350 --> 00:44:06.200
and the kind of macro estimates that you get
from those didn't prove to be very good guides.

00:44:06.200 --> 00:44:13.460
We probably got more of a response in terms
of the very sharp deceleration of investment

00:44:13.460 --> 00:44:16.710
in drilling and mining, for instance, than
we had seen in previous historical episodes.

00:44:16.710 --> 00:44:22.680
And it's hard to tell because these consumers
respond over time, but it also seems that

00:44:22.680 --> 00:44:30.109
consumers didn't respond as much as one might
have predicted based on older macro estimates.

00:44:30.109 --> 00:44:37.869
And similarly, just as I don't feel that the
historically rooted estimates were particularly

00:44:37.869 --> 00:44:43.859
accurate in thinking about the period in which
the oil price was declining, similarly now

00:44:43.859 --> 00:44:50.670
that we've seen a bit of an increase there
and a bit of a stabilization, I don't have

00:44:50.670 --> 00:44:59.059
a huge amount of confidence in how that is
going to show up in, you know, consumers responding

00:44:59.059 --> 00:45:04.240
to higher prices at the pump and therefore,
you know, seeing a little bit of a moderation

00:45:04.240 --> 00:45:11.670
on the consumption side, versus providing
more incentives for producers to go back and-certainly

00:45:11.670 --> 00:45:17.339
go back and start investing in exploration
and trying to expand capacity.

00:45:17.339 --> 00:45:21.930
I think it would be very hard to generalize.

00:45:21.930 --> 00:45:28.730
The other thing that's very important is that
our oil and gas sector has changed dramatically

00:45:28.730 --> 00:45:33.680
since some of those earlier estimates were
put in place.

00:45:33.680 --> 00:45:38.099
And so, again, what the price point is, I
don't think we have precise estimates.

00:45:38.099 --> 00:45:45.310
But certainly it's a very important driver,
and we're looking at it very closely.

00:45:45.310 --> 00:45:47.000
HILLS: Further questions?

00:45:47.000 --> 00:45:50.970
Yes, over at this table, right in front of
the mic.

00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:52.740
Q: Thank you.

00:45:52.740 --> 00:45:56.270
Governor Brainard, Rod von Lipsey, UBS.

00:45:56.270 --> 00:46:03.829
The question that I have just goes to your
sense in the Fed being data driven, but the

00:46:03.829 --> 00:46:10.190
economy and the markets being somewhat sentiment
driven, is there a possibility that we could

00:46:10.190 --> 00:46:14.160
have a sentiment-driven recession or trend?

00:46:14.160 --> 00:46:20.049
BRAINARD: So a part of the data we look at
relates to sentiment.

00:46:20.049 --> 00:46:21.240
(Laughter.)

00:46:21.240 --> 00:46:22.240
And you know, again.

00:46:22.240 --> 00:46:27.490
So if we look at some of the consumer-the
most recent consumer sentiment indicators,

00:46:27.490 --> 00:46:32.890
they actually look pretty resilient, which
is reassuring.

00:46:32.890 --> 00:46:43.820
But you know, there's different lags in terms
of how people respond to changes and their

00:46:43.820 --> 00:46:44.890
perceptions.

00:46:44.890 --> 00:46:50.819
And so, you know, we don't really know going
forward whether consumers are going to continue

00:46:50.819 --> 00:46:57.990
to feel buoyant and continue to see the kinds
of consumption numbers that we saw in April

00:46:57.990 --> 00:47:02.960
carrying through further into the second quarter,
which of course is very important factor in

00:47:02.960 --> 00:47:06.819
driving U.S. growth.

00:47:06.819 --> 00:47:19.069
But it is I think just an argument for being
a little bit patient in terms of reading more

00:47:19.069 --> 00:47:25.809
signals on the economy, not just on actual
spending but also on how both consumers and

00:47:25.809 --> 00:47:28.140
businesses are feeling about spending an investment.

00:47:28.140 --> 00:47:35.700
I think it just goes in the same direction,
which is that we want to have greater confidence

00:47:35.700 --> 00:47:41.450
that growth rebound is really in place before
making a policy judgement.

00:47:41.450 --> 00:47:44.790
HILLS: This table in the middle.

00:47:44.790 --> 00:47:45.790
Yes.

00:47:45.790 --> 00:47:49.670
Q: Thank you very much Sherry Stephenson,
International Centre for Trade and Sustainable

00:47:49.670 --> 00:47:51.380
Development, Geneva.

00:47:51.380 --> 00:47:54.290
I want to go back to the discussion on productivity.

00:47:54.290 --> 00:48:00.750
To me, that really seems to be a very, very
significant concern.

00:48:00.750 --> 00:48:06.319
The 1/2 percentage growth in productivity
that we've had since 2009 just isn't enough

00:48:06.319 --> 00:48:08.970
to have a very dynamic longer-term growth
prospect.

00:48:08.970 --> 00:48:13.700
And yet, you've stated that we don't know
the reasons behind this slowdown.

00:48:13.700 --> 00:48:18.109
So if we don't know the reasons, how can we
effectively address it, I mean, other than

00:48:18.109 --> 00:48:21.030
saying we need more business investment?

00:48:21.030 --> 00:48:26.440
And maybe that isn't an issue the Fed works
on, but could you comment on this and what

00:48:26.440 --> 00:48:27.440
you think?

00:48:27.440 --> 00:48:32.869
People say the IT-the spurt from the IT revolution
has now run its course and we don't have another

00:48:32.869 --> 00:48:36.299
big, you know, positive stimulus in sight.

00:48:36.299 --> 00:48:37.920
And that's of definite concern.

00:48:37.920 --> 00:48:42.279
So how do you boost long-term growth in the
absence of productivity gains?

00:48:42.279 --> 00:48:49.089
BRAINARD: So I-there are a bunch of reasons
that people are pointing to as possible explanations.

00:48:49.089 --> 00:48:56.319
And you know, I don't think any of them are
definitively-you know, in terms of the evidence-are

00:48:56.319 --> 00:48:59.160
definitively known to be the answer.

00:48:59.160 --> 00:49:05.619
And one of the reasons that's out there, as
you suggest, is that somehow the earlier investments

00:49:05.619 --> 00:49:12.760
that had been made in information technology
have now been fully diffused and run their

00:49:12.760 --> 00:49:13.880
course.

00:49:13.880 --> 00:49:20.170
Others are looking at the question of measurement
error, that we had some good work at the Fed

00:49:20.170 --> 00:49:25.119
which looks at this question of whether it's
possible that we're just not capturing some

00:49:25.119 --> 00:49:32.080
of those productivity gains because of the
way that we measure, services in particular.

00:49:32.080 --> 00:49:37.460
But of course, the question there is whether
that same measurement error wouldn't be equally

00:49:37.460 --> 00:49:41.089
applicable to earlier episodes with a lot
of IT.

00:49:41.089 --> 00:49:43.170
And so not clear.

00:49:43.170 --> 00:49:53.299
There are other questions or explanations,
that it takes a while for potentially productivity-enhancing

00:49:53.299 --> 00:49:55.200
investments to diffuse.

00:49:55.200 --> 00:50:01.580
So if you looked at artificial intelligence
today, or maybe robotics, or genetics, that

00:50:01.580 --> 00:50:07.380
maybe these are things that are quite promising,
but really haven't been diffused to any great

00:50:07.380 --> 00:50:08.380
degree.

00:50:08.380 --> 00:50:10.700
And so, you know, maybe we will see a pickup.

00:50:10.700 --> 00:50:12.559
I don't know the answer.

00:50:12.559 --> 00:50:16.869
I don't have a strong view one way or another.

00:50:16.869 --> 00:50:22.859
But it is obviously so critically important
in terms of driving potential that we need

00:50:22.859 --> 00:50:24.819
to understand it.

00:50:24.819 --> 00:50:28.520
And investment is a big piece of it.

00:50:28.520 --> 00:50:30.260
Education is an important piece.

00:50:30.260 --> 00:50:36.180
Having a very vibrant environment for innovation
not only to take place, but then to diffuse

00:50:36.180 --> 00:50:38.160
through investment is extremely important.

00:50:38.160 --> 00:50:43.710
So, you know, it is something that is very
important to our work.

00:50:43.710 --> 00:50:49.730
But again, you know, our tools in this area
are limited to the ones that try to create

00:50:49.730 --> 00:50:55.319
a-not create, but support a productive environment
for investment.

00:50:55.319 --> 00:50:57.400
HILLS: Yes, in the back.

00:50:57.400 --> 00:50:58.730
Way in the back.

00:50:58.730 --> 00:51:00.059
Q: Thank you.

00:51:00.059 --> 00:51:02.730
Jang Zhen (ph), Voice of America.

00:51:02.730 --> 00:51:07.670
Just wonder, what's the China factor in the
Fed's decision?

00:51:07.670 --> 00:51:16.160
Because there's talk about this rumor on Bloomberg
News, and talking about the S&amp;ED, Strategic

00:51:16.160 --> 00:51:19.010
and Economic Dialogue, which is being held
in Beijing.

00:51:19.010 --> 00:51:28.329
It said the officials in Beijing would just
ask the U.S. officials about the Fed interest

00:51:28.329 --> 00:51:32.940
rate hike issue, but Central Bank in China
denied it.

00:51:32.940 --> 00:51:39.400
But do you think that China-the Chinese economy
might be a factor?

00:51:39.400 --> 00:51:40.849
What do you think about this?

00:51:40.849 --> 00:51:41.849
Thanks.

00:51:41.849 --> 00:51:51.829
BRAINARD: So what I can say is that in assessing
the appropriate stance of monetary policy

00:51:51.829 --> 00:51:58.760
for the U.S., you know, we have a very clear
objective function that is established in

00:51:58.760 --> 00:52:08.910
law by Congress, which is domestic focused-full
employment and price stability, which has

00:52:08.910 --> 00:52:11.490
been interpreted as 2 percent inflation.

00:52:11.490 --> 00:52:13.970
So that's our focus.

00:52:13.970 --> 00:52:22.960
And then, of course, because we are so tightly
integrated into the world-you know, the dollar

00:52:22.960 --> 00:52:28.460
is viewed as a reserve currency, we have some
of the most liquid, deepest financial markets

00:52:28.460 --> 00:52:34.430
in the world-but of course we're also tightly
linked on the trade side.

00:52:34.430 --> 00:52:49.549
How our policy path and expectations of our
policy path are then affecting financial conditions,

00:52:49.549 --> 00:52:56.299
and of course importantly through exchange
rate channels, does, through a very important

00:52:56.299 --> 00:53:01.490
feedback loop, come back around and affect
U.S. domestic conditions.

00:53:01.490 --> 00:53:10.779
So in the past year, I would say, China has
figured prominently in terms of global risks

00:53:10.779 --> 00:53:18.250
and global financial conditions, as China
has managed challenges on its domestic front.

00:53:18.250 --> 00:53:23.319
And so those kinds of considerations naturally
have to be taken into account when we think

00:53:23.319 --> 00:53:24.770
about risks to the outlook.

00:53:24.770 --> 00:53:31.510
And they naturally will affect policy deliberations,
and potentially the path of policy.

00:53:31.510 --> 00:53:38.180
And so that is the way in which risks emanating
from China-and of course, you know, China

00:53:38.180 --> 00:53:45.589
was the really predominate source of global
investment growth for several years following

00:53:45.589 --> 00:53:47.950
the recovery.

00:53:47.950 --> 00:53:53.650
And that was a very important source of demand
and growth for emerging markets that were

00:53:53.650 --> 00:53:57.670
linked to China, both through commodity exports
as well as through supply chains.

00:53:57.670 --> 00:54:05.890
So we've seen that kind of as China's attempting
to rebalance its growth path-rely less on

00:54:05.890 --> 00:54:11.230
investment, less on resource-intensive, export-oriented
sectors, more on domestic demand-that has

00:54:11.230 --> 00:54:16.910
rippled through supply chains in other emerging
markets, and very importantly through commodity

00:54:16.910 --> 00:54:17.910
markets.

00:54:17.910 --> 00:54:25.210
And so those things do matter for our domestic
objectives back here in the U.S.

00:54:25.210 --> 00:54:27.990
HILLS: All right, question here.

00:54:27.990 --> 00:54:33.270
Q: Irving Williamson, U.S. International Trade
Commission.

00:54:33.270 --> 00:54:36.150
You touched on fiscal policy in Europe and
in Asia.

00:54:36.150 --> 00:54:39.910
I was wondering if you might want to touch
on fiscal policy in the U.S., and how that

00:54:39.910 --> 00:54:44.930
might be complicating your efforts, particularly
in regards to the question Mr. Cunningham

00:54:44.930 --> 00:54:50.680
raise about income inequality in the U.S.,
achieving full employment when you see the

00:54:50.680 --> 00:54:53.900
number of people dropping out of the wage-the
labor force increasing.

00:54:53.900 --> 00:54:55.279
Thank you.

00:54:55.279 --> 00:55:03.619
BRAINARD: So in the U.S., and we are differently
situated than many of our foreign partners

00:55:03.619 --> 00:55:11.369
in the sense that we have been recovering
and, you know, we have made quite a lot of

00:55:11.369 --> 00:55:16.369
progress on the employment leg of our mandate.

00:55:16.369 --> 00:55:21.450
You know, the labor market has come a long
way although, again, we've seen some signs

00:55:21.450 --> 00:55:27.440
of slowing in the last few months.

00:55:27.440 --> 00:55:34.160
A little bit less progress, but nonetheless,
you know, we've also seen core inflation around

00:55:34.160 --> 00:55:37.640
1.6 percent, which is below our objective.

00:55:37.640 --> 00:55:41.250
But we are seeing progress.

00:55:41.250 --> 00:55:48.630
And so we are differently situated relative
to some of the other economies, particularly

00:55:48.630 --> 00:55:51.770
Japan and the euro area.

00:55:51.770 --> 00:55:58.910
Nonetheless, you know, in a period where in
particular, you know, it may be that the neutral

00:55:58.910 --> 00:56:05.200
rate is low for some period of time, and a
period where, you know, there are reasons

00:56:05.200 --> 00:56:11.150
to want to both support aggregate demand in
the short run, and also the productive potential

00:56:11.150 --> 00:56:16.000
of the economy and productivity in the longer
run, there may be a case to be made, for instance,

00:56:16.000 --> 00:56:23.339
for infrastructure investment complementing
the goals that we're trying to achieve in

00:56:23.339 --> 00:56:25.769
monetary policy.

00:56:25.769 --> 00:56:32.250
But again, you know, those questions about
really needing to bolster aggregate demand

00:56:32.250 --> 00:56:39.630
are even more important in other parts of
the world where they have a bigger challenge.

00:56:39.630 --> 00:56:42.440
HILLS: Well, I'm afraid we've run out of time.

00:56:42.440 --> 00:56:50.670
But I hope you'll join me in thanking Dr.
Brainard for not only a wonderful presentation,

00:56:50.670 --> 00:56:55.470
but also the most comprehensive responses
to a great variety of questions.

00:56:55.470 --> 00:56:57.431
So thank you so much for taking your time
to be with us.

00:56:57.431 --> 00:56:58.431
(Applause.)

00:56:58.431 --> 00:56:58.432
(END)

